1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,474
When floods hit Vietnam, millions are donated
within days.

2
00:00:04,527 --> 00:00:10,256
ReliefCloak lets residents report needs
privately, and lets donors see where every dong
goes.

3
00:00:10,316 --> 00:00:12,480
The problem is trust.

4
00:00:12,546 --> 00:00:19,284
In a normal charity app, one admin who controls
the server can grant themselves payout rights
and send money anywhere.

5
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:23,354
Here, a resident donates and gets exactly one
receipt.

6
00:00:25,724 --> 00:00:28,323
Payouts need three different people.

7
00:00:28,537 --> 00:00:34,400
An admin proposes, an independent auditor
approves, and a separate officer executes.

8
00:00:36,919 --> 00:00:41,753
Every step lands in a public ledger, without
donor or recipient identities.

9
00:00:42,337 --> 00:00:45,416
This is where TideCloak changes the rules.

10
00:00:46,135 --> 00:00:52,814
Every sign-in returns a signed token, and every
API verifies it. A forged role is rejected.

11
00:00:52,871 --> 00:00:57,695
Role changes are change requests that only
commit when a quorum of admins signs.

12
00:00:57,749 --> 00:01:02,658
Minh tries to give himself payout rights. It
stays at one of two.

13
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:06,029
Ha, a second admin, denies it.

14
00:01:07,269 --> 00:01:11,018
Granting a new officer works only when two
admins agree.

15
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:24,164
No single admin holds that power alone.

16
00:01:25,089 --> 00:01:28,223
Donors no longer have to trust one person.

17
00:01:28,286 --> 00:01:37,169
TideCloak is simulated here because the Tide
network was overloaded. The same tokens, roles
and quorum switch to live TideCloak with one
setting.

18
00:01:37,228 --> 00:01:41,652
ReliefCloak. Emergent authority for disaster
relief.

